 "relevance_note": "A 1000-patient Middle East genomic-diagnosis cohort; one patient row in the supplementary positive-findings table carries a TNFAIP3 variant: \"TNFAIP3 (NM_001270508.1): c.1939A>C; p.(Thr647Pro)\" with \"Autoinflammatory syndrome, familial, Behcet-like\".",
 "method_note": "Read the whole Markdown by hand; no script transcribed a table (a script only assembled the JSON). Only two lines mention TNFAIP3: the Table S5-style disease count row ('| TNFAIP3 | Autoinflammatory syndrome, familial, Behcet-like | 1 |') and one row of the supplementary sequencing-variant table. The supplementary file is an excerpt: the title row of that variant table is cut by '[...]'. It is labelled 'Table S6' by inference only (it is the sequencing positive-findings table directly before Table S7, and the main text says the sequencing positive findings are in 'Additional file 1: Table S6'); the row number 42 counts data rows from the first data row shown in the excerpt (the table runs contiguously, no '[...]' inside it). The table has no patient-label or case-number column, so the person gets a rule-3 label. Columns: Mode of Inheritance (two merged columns), Type of NGS testing, Variants (cDNA; protein), ACMG-AMP codes, Genomic Coordinates, Novel or Reported, Zygosity, Variant Origin, Syndrome/Disease, Management and Intervention. Variant Origin is 'NA', so inheritance and parental testing are not reported. 'Novel' is a novelty status, not a pathogenicity class, so pathogenicity is not reported (the table title, which might say diagnostic/pathogenic, is not in the excerpt). Variant type is not stated (not derived from HGVS). 'AD' in the Mode of Inheritance column is the disease's inheritance mode, not this person's variant origin. is_proband is taken from the paper's statement that all 1000 patients are probands ('Of the 1000 probands'). The paper gives no sex, age, ancestry or individual phenotype for this person, so there are no phenotypic_features rows and all 20 checklist items are not reported. cohort_size_as_stated = 1000; n_patients_reported = 1 from the disease-count table. No contradictions found.",
